047 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551047 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc8f5a46-48bc-535e-8332-02ed609bb690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551138 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7abffeb2-f396-51d3-8c57-6dd2f44d6299" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551169 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a97b574a-ddca-51ce-b670-8bb1bab44ffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186551020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551219 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efdbde40-612d-561b-91ae-eae8bf3a3a21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551289 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551289 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551289 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551289 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, what is the highest possible degree of $P+Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551289 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70cb115a-1a5a-5899-8751-fbde76c6f208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551289 #edb/ref 1074 "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $5.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{5}$ and $Q={x}^{5}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{5}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{5},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551326 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551326 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $5$, respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nII. $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b45d9b53-e4ec-561f-955c-4e4b1f88b215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551326 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{5}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551335 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551335 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551335 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551335 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $P(x)-4{x}^{6}$ is a polynomial\n\nII. $4{x}^{6}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{P(x)}$ is a polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551335 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ceee5cde-40e3-565c-b032-fa1a9c5be489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551335 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{5}+1.$ Then $\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{P(x)}=\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}+1},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551371 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b7d2c5a-9dd1-516d-a496-880f67c4ba7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551371 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186551289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551396 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186551446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551396 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47e18e53-ae77-5c80-aa5d-573b4263a98c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551396 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186551326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551446 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186551371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551446 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c430f29a-849f-5c9d-b6d0-59cdd7fd14de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551446 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186551335 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
